<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mircea the Elder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1418">1418</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Mircea I of Wallachia, Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mircea the Elder was prince of Wallachia from 1386 to 1418, with a short interruption in the 1390s. He extended his rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Rovine in 1395, where Marko Kraljević fell on the Ottoman side, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of Nicopolis in 1396. Later he intervened in the Ottoman civil war before agreeing to pay tribute to the sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder.jpg" alt="Mircea the Elder" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MirceatheElder.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mircea the Elder (Romanian: Mircea cel Bătrân) was born about 1355 and died on 31 January 1418. He was a son of Radu I of Wallachia and his wife Calinichia, and so a descendant of the House of Basarab. He inherited the throne in 1386 after the death of his step-brother Dan I of Wallachia. Wallachia was then developing economically, administratively and religiously, and was exposed to the ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, which sought to control the mouths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and to those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p>The byname elder was given after his death to distinguish him from his grandson Mircea II, though some historians think later generations used it as a mark of respect. Among his sons were Michael I of Wallachia and Vlad II Dracul, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlad-the-impaler">Vlad Țepeș</a> was his grandson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under Mircea Wallachia controlled the largest area in its history. It gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> in 1388 and the Banate of Severin in 1388 or 1389, and he held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> of Amlaș and Făgăraș in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. He organised the high offices of state, raised revenue, and minted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins that circulated beyond the country. He gave trade privileges to merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, renewed those of Brașov, fortified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> citadels and strengthened an army of townspeople and free and dependent peasants. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> and built the Cozia Monastery about 1390.</p><p>Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petru-i-of-moldavia">Petru Mușat</a> of Moldavia he concluded an alliance in 1389 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, renewed in 1404 and 1410, and he kept close relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-holy-roman-emperor">Sigismund of Luxembourg</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><p>Mircea's support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> brought conflict. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> with about 40,000 men against Mircea's roughly 10,000. Mircea waged a war of starvation and small attacks, and the armies met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rovine">Battle of Rovine</a> on forested, swampy ground that kept the Ottomans from deploying their army. Mircea then had to retreat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and the Ottomans installed Vlad the Usurper. In 1396 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> begun by the king of Hungary, which ended in Ottoman victory at Nicopolis on 25 September.</p><p>In 1397, having defeated Vlad with help from Stibor, voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, he stopped another Ottoman expedition, and he defeated a further one in 1400. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> defeated Bayezid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> in 1402, Mircea exploited the Ottoman anarchy, regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a> in 1404 and helped Musa ascend the Ottoman throne, reaching the height of his power. Late in his reign he agreed to pay a tribute of 3,000 gold pieces a year, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> did not make Wallachia a province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mircea the Elder” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…at the Battle of Posada in 1330, held a precarious autonomy under Ottoman suzerainty for four centuries, resisted under <b>Mircea the Elder</b>, Vlad the Impaler and Michael the Brave, passed through Phanariote and Russian tutelage, and in 1859 united with Moldavia to form the state that became Romania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</b> met the invading Ottoman army of Sultan Bayezid I, which heavily outnumbered his own and included Serbian vassals, at Rovine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-17">17 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</b> held part of Dobruja briefly before it passed fully to the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-dobruja">Despotate of Dobruja</a></li><li><blockquote>Marko died fighting for the Ottomans against <b>Mircea the Elder of Wallachia</b> at Rovine in 1395, and his lands were annexed by the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-prilep">Kingdom of Prilep</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Curtea de Argeș, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Curtea de Argeș, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.12,24.67&amp;year=1408" aria-label="Curtea de Argeș on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mircea the Elder"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4679 6246h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4679 6246h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.12,24.67&amp;year=1408">What was here in 1408? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
